    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p38-q16-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that there is a bijection from $(0,1]$ to $[0,1]$. Deduce that to prove $[0,1]$ and $[0,1]^2$ have the same cardinality, it suffices to prove $(0,1]$ and $(0,1]^2$ have the same cardinality.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Send $1$ to $0$, send $\\frac{1}{n}$ to $\\frac{1}{(n-1)}$ for $n\\ge2$, and fix all other points.\n\nApply this bijection to each coordinate, then compose with a bijection $(0,1]\\to(0,1]^2$.",
          "worked": "Define $h:(0,1]\\to[0,1]$ by\n$$h(x)=\\begin{cases}0,&x=1,\\\\1/(n-1),&x=1/n,\\ n\\ge2,\\\\x,&x\\notin\\{1/n:n\\in\\mathbb N\\}.\\end{cases}$$\nOn the reciprocal sequence this shifts $1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3},\\ldots$ onto $0,1,\\frac{1}{2},\\ldots$. All other points are fixed. These two ranges are disjoint and together cover $[0,1]$, with each point having exactly one preimage. Thus $h$ is bijective.\nThe map $H:(0,1]^2\\to[0,1]^2$, $H(x,y)=(h(x),h(y))$, is also bijective. If $f:(0,1]\\to(0,1]^2$ is bijective, then $H\\circ f\\circ h^{-1}$ is a bijection $[0,1]\\to[0,1]^2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 70
      },
      {
        "id": "p38-q16-map-context",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p39-q-cont-map",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "We now define a map",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "f:(0,1]\\to(0,1]^2",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p39-q-cont-definition-text",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "This mapping involves splitting decimal representations of numbers into blocks that contain some zeroes (possibly none) followed by a single non-zero digit, and then allocating these blocks alternately to the left and right in ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "(0,1]^2",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ". We demonstrate this with an example:",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p39-q-cont-example-text",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "center",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "\\begin{aligned}f(0.{\\color{#ef6068}03}\\,{\\color{#268cff}001}\\,{\\color{#ef6068}5}\\,{\\color{#268cff}6}\\,{\\color{#ef6068}07}\\,{\\color{#268cff}2}\\,{\\color{#ef6068}005}\\ldots)\\\\=\\bigl(0.{\\color{#ef6068}03507005}\\ldots,\\,0.{\\color{#268cff}00162}\\ldots\\bigr)\\end{aligned}",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p39-q-cont-convention",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "In order for our mapping to be well-defined, we avoid decimal representations involving infinite strings of zeroes. For example, we represent ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "\\tfrac12",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " as ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "0.4999\\ldots",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ", instead of ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "0.5000\\ldots",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "responseSpace": "given",
        "answerSpaceMm": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "p39-q-cont-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "If we had defined our mapping to simply split the decimal representation by allocating consecutive digits alternately to the left and right in $(0,1]^2$, then it would not be bijective. Give an example to show this.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$0.45909090\\ldots$ and $0.44999999\\ldots$ both give $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, so digit splitting is not injective.",
          "worked": "Splitting alternate digits gives\n$$0.45909090\\ldots\\longmapsto(0.4999\\ldots,0.5000\\ldots)=(1/2,1/2),$$\nwhereas\n$$0.44999999\\ldots\\longmapsto(0.4999\\ldots,0.4999\\ldots)=(1/2,1/2).$$\nThe two inputs are different but their outputs are equal. Therefore this map is not injective and cannot be bijective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p39-q-cont-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Explain why $f$ is bijective, and deduce that $[0,1]$ and $[0,1]^2$ have the same cardinality.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The inverse alternately interleaves the blocks of the two coordinates. Thus $f$ is bijective; composing with the endpoint bijections in part (a) gives $|[0,1]|=|[0,1]^2|$.",
          "worked": "Use the decimal expansion that is not eventually zero; for $1$, use $0.999\\ldots$. Each expansion has infinitely many non-zero digits and therefore a unique sequence of blocks, each ending at the next non-zero digit.\nWrite an input as $0.A_1A_2A_3\\ldots$, where the $A_i$ are these blocks. Then\n$$f(0.A_1A_2A_3\\ldots)=(0.A_1A_3A_5\\ldots,0.A_2A_4A_6\\ldots).$$\nEach coordinate still has infinitely many non-zero digits, so its chosen expansion and its block decomposition are unique. Equal output coordinates therefore give equal odd blocks and equal even blocks, hence equal inputs. Thus $f$ is injective.\nFor any output pair $(0.B_1B_2\\ldots,0.C_1C_2\\ldots)$, interleave its blocks to form $0.B_1C_1B_2C_2\\ldots$. This is an element of $(0,1]$, and its image is the required pair. Thus $f$ is surjective.\nConsequently $f$ is bijective, and part (a) gives $|[0,1]|=|[0,1]^2|$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p39-q-cont-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Use the ideas introduced above to explain why $\\mathbb R$ and $\\mathbb R^n$ have the same cardinality for any $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Transfer the block bijection to obtain a bijection $\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$, then combine one coordinate at a time by induction.",
          "worked": "There is a bijection $j:(0,1]\\to(0,1)$: send $\\frac{1}{n}$ to $\\frac{1}{(n+1)}$ for every $n\\ge1$, and fix all other points. The reciprocal sequence maps onto all reciprocal points in $(0,1)$, while the remaining points map identically.\nThe function $t(x)=\\tan(\\pi x-\\frac{\\pi}{2})$ is a bijection $(0,1)\\to\\mathbb R$. Hence $k=t\\circ j$ is a bijection $(0,1]\\to\\mathbb R$.\nLet $K(x,y)=(k(x),k(y))$. Using part (c),\n$$F=k\\circ f^{-1}\\circ K^{-1}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$$\nis a bijection.\nFor $n=1$ use the identity. If $G_n:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is bijective, define\n$$G_{n+1}(x_1,\\ldots,x_{n+1})=F(G_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n),x_{n+1}).$$\nThis is a composition of bijections, so it is bijective. Induction establishes $|\\mathbb R^n|=|\\mathbb R|$ for every $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      }
    ]
